{"note":"AUTO-GENERATED by tools/export_plateau.mjs. World metres: x east, y up, z south.","geo":{"lat0":29.9791687,"lon0":31.1342125,"mPerDegLon":96426.2,"mPerDegLat":110852,"datumASL":59.6},"extent":{"x0":-1500,"x1":1100,"z0":-700,"z1":1500},"grid":{"x0":-1500,"z0":-700,"step":10,"nx":261,"nz":221},"pyramids":[{"id":"khufu","x":0,"z":0,"baseY":0,"base":230.4,"height":146.6},{"id":"khafre","x":-334.41132,"z":353.86264,"baseY":8.7,"base":215.25,"height":143.5},{"id":"menkaure","x":-574.4463999999999,"z":739.1908,"baseY":11.8,"base":102.2,"height":65}],"sphinx":{"x":326.1,"z":431,"floorY":-42.5},"teleports":[{"label":"Plateau Overlook (start)","pos":[-120,-1.39,-215]},{"label":"Great Pyramid — base, north face","pos":[3.6,1.05,-137.87]},{"label":"Pyramid of Khafre — north base","pos":[-339.41,9.7,231.86]},{"label":"Pyramid of Menkaure — north base","pos":[-581.45,12.8,679.19]},{"label":"The Great Sphinx","pos":[366.6,-42,431]},{"label":"Tomb of Khentkaus I","pos":[120.9,-31.47,603]},{"label":"Workers' Town","pos":[555,-39.79,843]},{"label":"Menkaure Valley Temple","pos":[207.7,-36.65,704.5]},{"label":"Khafre Valley Temple","pos":[363.1,-41.45,498]},{"label":"Wall of the Crow (gate)","pos":[557.45,-39.79,766.25]},{"label":"Khufu Valley Temple","pos":[855.56,-37.44,-145.33]},{"label":"Trial Passages","pos":[160,-0.01,-54]},{"label":"Workers' Cemetery","pos":[395,-11.28,875]},{"label":"Central Field Quarry","pos":[-40,-12.83,534]},{"label":"Temple of Amenhotep II","pos":[369.9,-36.68,373.6]},{"label":"Menkaure Quarry","pos":[-359.4,-4.35,802.1]},{"label":"Panorama Viewpoint (south-west)","pos":[-737,15.14,1149]}],"landmarks":[{"name":"Great Pyramid of Khufu","blurb":"Built c. 2560 BC for Pharaoh Khufu. 2.3 million blocks, originally 146.6 m tall — the tallest human structure for ~3,800 years.","radius":130,"pos":[0,146.6,0]},{"name":"Pyramid of Khafre","blurb":"Built c. 2570 BC for Khafre. Appears taller than Khufu's pyramid only because it stands on higher bedrock. Keeps its original Tura limestone casing near the summit.","radius":130,"pos":[-334.41,152.2,353.86]},{"name":"Pyramid of Menkaure","blurb":"Built c. 2510 BC for Menkaure, the smallest of the three. Its lowest courses were clad in red Aswan granite.","radius":130,"pos":[-574.45,76.8,739.19]},{"name":"The Great Sphinx","blurb":"A 73 m limestone sphinx with the body of a lion and a royal human head, generally attributed to the reign of Khafre.","radius":70,"pos":[326.1,-30.5,431]},{"name":"Sphinx Temple","blurb":"Built of blocks quarried from around the Sphinx itself — an open court ringed by granite-cased pillars, aligned with the equinox sunrise.","radius":30,"pos":[392.9,-38,437.1]},{"name":"Khafre Valley Temple","blurb":"Khafre's granite-clad valley temple beside the Sphinx, at the foot of his causeway — its pillared hall held his great seated statues.","radius":34,"pos":[395.1,-37.6,492]},{"name":"Temple of Amenhotep II","blurb":"A mud-brick chapel built ~1,100 years after the pyramids by Amenhotep II, who worshipped the Sphinx as Horemakhet, \"Horus in the Horizon\".","radius":20,"pos":[375.9,-37.7,395.6]},{"name":"Khufu Mortuary Temple","blurb":"Only the black basalt pavement of Khufu's mortuary temple survives — its limestone walls were carried off as building stone.","radius":30,"pos":[135.2,3,0]},{"name":"Khafre Mortuary Temple","blurb":"The most complete Old Kingdom mortuary temple: entrance hall, pillared court and five statue niches, built of colossal local limestone blocks.","radius":40,"pos":[-161.3,7.88,352.5]},{"name":"Menkaure Valley Temple","blurb":"Menkaure's valley temple at the foot of his causeway — begun in stone and finished in mud brick after his death.","radius":32,"pos":[207.7,-33.4,738.5]},{"name":"Khufu Valley Temple","blurb":"Khufu's valley temple at the east end of his causeway — its remains lie buried beneath the modern village of Nazlet el-Samman.","radius":30,"pos":[879.56,-35.88,-151.33]},{"name":"Central Field Quarry (GIS)","blurb":"The main quarry south of the Great Pyramid, where workers cut the core limestone blocks — leaving stepped benches in the bedrock.","radius":80,"pos":[-40,-18.95,590]},{"name":"Menkaure Quarry","blurb":"Quarry faces and channels south-east of Menkaure — the source of his core blocks, still showing the trenches where stones were levered free.","radius":90,"pos":[-359.4,-6.93,884.1]},{"name":"Tomb of Khentkaus I","blurb":"The \"Fourth Pyramid of Giza\" — the two-tiered rock-cut tomb of Queen Khentkaus I, at the head of the Central Field.","radius":40,"pos":[120.9,-24.8,641]},{"name":"Workers' Town (Heit el-Ghurab)","blurb":"Heit el-Ghurab, the \"Lost City of the Pyramids\": barracks, bakeries and long galleries that housed the crews who built Giza.","radius":110,"pos":[585,-36.89,905]},{"name":"Wall of the Crow","blurb":"A massive limestone wall (~200 m long, ~10 m high) with a monumental gateway between the plateau and the workers' town.","radius":60,"pos":[557.45,-35.05,782.25]},{"name":"Khufu Satellite Pyramid (G1-d)","blurb":"A small satellite (cult) pyramid for the king's ka.","radius":25.400000000000002,"pos":[95,13.8,130]},{"name":"Khafre Satellite Pyramid (G II-a)","blurb":"A small satellite (cult) pyramid for the king's ka.","radius":24.080000000000002,"pos":[-333.5,19.8,498]},{"name":"Khufu's Solar Boat","blurb":"A nod to the 43 m cedar \"solar barque\" found dismantled in a sealed pit beside the Great Pyramid in 1954 and painstakingly reassembled.","radius":26,"pos":[-30,5,140]},{"name":"Trial Passages","blurb":"Rock-cut passages east of the Great Pyramid that reproduce its internal corridors at near-full scale — likely a builders' mock-up.","radius":22,"pos":[160,1.32,-42]},{"name":"Khentkaus Town","blurb":"A settlement of priests who served the cult of Queen Khentkaus I, built against her monument.","radius":45,"pos":[178,-31,662]},{"name":"Workers' Cemetery","blurb":"Tombs of the pyramid builders on the slope above their town — small mud-brick and stone tombs, some with miniature vaulted superstructures.","radius":55,"pos":[395,-6.66,905]}],"routes":[[-1353,690.5,-1302.5,671,-1230.5,657.5,-1051.5,643.5,-829,639.5,-795,622.5,-751.5,580.5,-726,563.5,-678.5,543,-589,517.5,-578,503,-566.5,437,-562.5,291.5,-552,190,-532,134],[-1162,181.5,-1138,211,-1104,207.5,-1025,235.5,-1009,248,-986,275.5,-973,281,-919,276,-850.5,280,-815.5,241,-792.5,226],[-1039.5,89,-1038.5,121,-1033.5,126.5,-880.5,118,-868.5,122,-831.5,146],[-934.5,121,-932.5,159.5,-929.5,165,-878,165,-864.5,157.5,-850.5,123,-856,41,-862,37,-932.5,40.5,-958.5,54.5],[-659,536.5,-687.5,484,-782.5,384,-841,302,-855.5,294],[-162,-311,-153.5,-320,-151,-332,-155.5,-343.5,-166,-351,-249,-358,-283.5,-373.5,-299.5,-369,-307.5,-358.5,-306.5,-336.5,-293,-324.5,-233,-320,-191.5,-307.5,-162,-311],[566,593,566,465.5,580,419,573.5,399.5,558,387.5,528.5,380,414,378.5,299,349,232.5,321.5,138,265,33,222,-13.5,208.5,-104.5,200,-117,191,-131.5,166,-136.5,138,-136.5,-117,-123.5,-133.5,-73,-157.5,-54,-170.5,-39.5,-187,-34,-202.5,-44,-226,-100.5,-291],[353.5,476.5,356.5,528,363.5,538,435,550,515.5,554.5,519.5,558.5,519.5,604.5,535.5,610.5],[608,635.5,600,629.5,538.5,637.5,532.5,641.5,530.5,648,543.5,716.5,553,719,614,706.5,618,698,608,635.5]],"rideables":[{"x":356.1,"z":383,"rot":1,"saddle":11546672},{"x":372.1,"z":375,"rot":1.4,"saddle":2972320},{"x":40,"z":-165,"rot":0.4,"saddle":14196784},{"x":52,"z":-172,"rot":0.9,"saddle":4161343},{"x":-725,"z":1140,"rot":2.2,"saddle":11546672},{"x":-740,"z":1132,"rot":2.6,"saddle":9060256}],"artifacts":[{"id":"lid","type":"fragment","name":"Sarcophagus lid fragment","blurb":"A corner of the red-granite lid that was already gone when Caliph al-Ma'mun's men forced their way in (AD 820).","pos":[4.29,43.12,10.45]},{"id":"copper","type":"tool","name":"Copper fitting from the shaft","blurb":"Like the copper 'handles' found on the door-slab at the top of the Queen's Chamber shaft by the Upuaut robot in 1993.","pos":[6.54,21.53,-0.83]},{"id":"lamp","type":"jar","name":"Tomb robber's oil lamp","blurb":"A pottery saucer lamp of the kind carried by the medieval explorers who scrambled down to the unfinished Subterranean Chamber.","pos":[8.29,-30.07,3.82]},{"id":"chisel","type":"tool","name":"Quarryman's copper chisel","blurb":"Copper chisels and dolerite pounders were the only metal tools available; they were resharpened constantly.","pos":[7.29,14.82,-96.87]},{"id":"ushabti","type":"ushabti","name":"Ushabti of Thutmose IV","blurb":"Thutmose IV cleared the Sphinx of sand around 1400 BC after a dream, and raised the Dream Stele between its paws.","pos":[356.1,-42.05,432.5]},{"id":"diorite","type":"scarab","name":"Diorite chip from a Khafre statue","blurb":"Khafre's famous seated statue (Cairo Museum) was found buried in a pit in this valley temple in 1860.","pos":[367.1,-41.13,500]},{"id":"triad","type":"fragment","name":"Greywacke triad fragment","blurb":"Reisner found Menkaure's triad statues — the king with Hathor and a nome goddess — here in 1908.","pos":[210.7,-36.5,702.5]},{"id":"bedja","type":"jar","name":"Bread mould (bedja)","blurb":"Thousands of these conical bread pots were found in the bakeries of the workers' town beyond the Wall of the Crow.","pos":[560.45,-39.47,770.25]},{"id":"beer","type":"jar","name":"Beer jar","blurb":"Workers were paid in bread and beer — roughly ten loaves and a jug or two a day.","pos":[559,-39.6,846]},{"id":"amulet","type":"amulet","name":"Faience amulet","blurb":"Blue-green faience amulets were buried with the priests who served Queen Khentkaus' cult.","pos":[123.9,-31.32,608]},{"id":"square","type":"tool","name":"Wooden set-square","blurb":"The Trial Passages were the builders' full-size mock-up; set-squares and plumb lines kept the angles true.","pos":[162,0.28,-51]},{"id":"basalt","type":"fragment","name":"Basalt paving block","blurb":"Black basalt from the Fayum paved the floors of Khufu's temples — the same stone as the causeway pavement.","pos":[851.56,-37.12,-142.33]},{"id"